/**
`(N)` — the same tag, written N times.

`(N)` closes a tag's head: after the name, the shorthands and the attributes,
before the text. The tag is written that many times, with everything under it —
`li.item(3)` is three list items, and a tag with a subtree is repeated whole.

    star(5)
    div.card(2)
        p inside

The count is an expression, so it may be computed: `li(len(items))`. A literal
count is written out as copies; anything else becomes a counted loop, since the
number is only known when the template runs.

The `(` has to sit against the tag, with no space before it. A tag's text may
open with a parenthesis of its own — `a[href="tel:…"] (781) 496-8660` — and the
space is what keeps it text.

The formatter goes the other way: a run of sibling tags that write out the same
is folded into one `(N)`. It compares what they *write*, so two subtrees fold
only when they are the same down to the last attribute, and only up to the
number of copies the lowering writes out — past that a `(N)` is a loop, and
folding would change the template rather than only how it is written.
**/

@param (; stars=4)

@main
    hr(3)
    div.rating
        i.star(5)
    ul
        li.item(2)
            b two of these
    div.dynamic
        i.star(stars)
    p (this stays text) — the space before the parenthesis is what says so
